/**

\page InteractionHowTo Interaction Related Examples in MITK

\tableofcontents

\section EnableDisableInteraction How to keep Display Interaction from reacting to the left mouse button

Display interaction (setting the crosshair, panning, zooming, scrolling, ...) is implemented by
mitk::DisplayActionEventBroadcast, one per multi widget, each registered as an mitk::InteractionEventObserver.
Observers are notified about every event that no mitk::DataInteractor handled. A tool that is an observer
itself, like the segmentation tools, therefore gets the same left mouse button press as the display
interaction, and a single click would both draw and move the crosshair.

Such a tool blocks left mouse button presses for display actions while it is active:

\code
#include <mitkDisplayActionEventBroadcast.h>

// Member
mitk::DisplayActionEventBroadcast::LeftButtonBlock m_DisplayLeftButtonBlock;

// On activation
m_DisplayLeftButtonBlock = mitk::DisplayActionEventBroadcast::BlockLeftButton();

// On deactivation (destroying the block releases it as well)
m_DisplayLeftButtonBlock.Reset();
\endcode

The block applies to all broadcasts, including broadcasts created while it is held, and switching the
interaction scheme does not lift it. Blocks are counted, so independent tools do not need to coordinate
their blocks. All other display interaction, like the mouse wheel, the middle and right mouse buttons,
and the keyboard, stays available.

Tools derived from mitk::Tool do not hold a block themselves but switch on BlocksDisplayLeftButton in
their constructor; mitk::SegTool2D does this for all 2D segmentation tools.

A mitk::DataInteractor does not need a block for events it handles, since handled events are not passed
on to the display interaction.

*/
